WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.294
In the modern
era, Batman is one of the most popular

00:00:03.294 --> 00:00:06.297
superheroes
to ever exist in the history of DC Comics,

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:09.551
and I would go so far as to argue he's
more popular than Superman.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:14.806
What's so funny about this
is Batman is not supposed to exist

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:18.351
because there was a time when DC Comics

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.895
was going to cancel his comic book line

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:23.857
and kill him off forever.

00:00:24.899 --> 00:00:25.817
So when Batman first

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.029
came onto the scene in 1939,
his stories were dark.

00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:33.742
His early comics saw him killing villains
with poison gas, hanging mental patients

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:37.370
from the bat wing, watching on while
villains burned to death, and even showing

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.373
the Joker
assassinating a political official.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:43.543
Sure, they were morally questionable acts,
but his no kill

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.546
rule had not been established
by this point, and the general argument

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:49.257
was that the villains had it coming
anyway.

00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:52.218
But all of this changed in the mid
to late 1950s.

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:56.222
The reason for this was due to a pseudo
psychologist named Frederic Wertham,

00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:59.434
who published a book in 1954
called Seduction of the Innocent,

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:03.438
arguing the comic books were corrupting
America's youth, Wertham’s

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.108
book drew the attention of both
the American People and Casey Kefauver,

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:10.570
a senator from Tennessee,
who saw the book as a rallying cry.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.947
As a result of the general public's
reaction,

00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:14.991
as well as his own political aspirations,

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:18.745
Casey brought Wertham before the Senate
Subcommittee on Juvenile Delinquency

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:20.538
and Wertham’s testimony

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:24.751
nearly led to Congress passing legislation
that would have severely restricted

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:26.878
what could be published
within a comic book.

00:01:26.878 --> 00:01:28.296
But despite the testimonies

00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:32.050
of Comic Creators, Publishers
and other Psychologists who saw Wertham’s

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:36.054
book as an example of cherry picking data,
something had to be done.

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:39.224
So the comic industry chose to regulate

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:42.227
itself,
establishing the Comics Code Authority.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.688
The ins and outs of the Comics
Code are outside the scope of this video,

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:50.193
but any comic that did not contain
the Comics Code Seal of Approval

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:53.363
would not be sold,
and among the various rules of the Comics

00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:56.574
Code was the rule
that vigilantism could not be glorified

00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:59.202
and depictions of extreme violence
couldn't be shown.

00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:00.620
But what was Batman,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.415
if not a comic book character glorifying
violence and using extreme tactics?

00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:07.794
So to ensure that Batman comics could be
sold,

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:10.755
DC was forced to tone his character down.

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:14.300
His dark and hyper violent ways
were replaced with goofy moments

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.638
like the Rainbow and Zebra Batman suits
in Detective Comics 241 and 275,

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:23.393
respectively, the sword fight between
Batman and Superman in World's Finest 143

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.854
and the story of Batman
wearing a jester hat

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:29.149
and punching the side of a building
in a World’s Finest 109.

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:32.443
Being such a stark difference
from the stories that so many had grown up

00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:35.738
with meant that Batman's audience
had begun to dwindle.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:40.160
Fans were angry and they were betrayed
in that the antithesis

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.830
to Superman's Boy Scout mentality
had been reduced to a goof.

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:48.835
But with their hands tied by the comics
code, what was DC Comics supposed to do?

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:57.302
The Adam West Batman

00:02:57.302 --> 00:03:01.306
show was actually the saving grace
the DC Comics needed.

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:04.017
Originally intended
to be a juvenile adventure show.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:08.521
The television rights for Batman shifted
from CBS to ABC, and then eventually FOX,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.899
who hired William Dozier
to produce the series,

00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:14.611
but after reading several of the existing
Batman stories under the rules

00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:17.614
of the Comics Code,
Dozier decided to make the series campy,

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:20.658
focusing more on nonsensical
slapstick humor.

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:24.370
But much like during the height
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, viewers

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.373
who had never read a Batman comic
in their life

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:30.877
saw themselves flocking to local stores
to pick up the newest issue

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.672
of the Caped Crusader,
and for a time, sales skyrocketed.

00:03:35.298 --> 00:03:40.803
In fact, between 1966 and 1968
when the show aired, Batman's comics

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:45.683
went from selling 400,000 issues
per month to almost 900,000.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.727
And it made perfect sense.

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:49.812
The 1960s

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:52.815
were a socially chaotic time in America.

00:03:52.941 --> 00:03:57.111
Society saw some of the highest highs
with NASA's moon landing and Beatlemania,

00:03:57.528 --> 00:03:59.322
but they also saw the lowest lows

00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:02.325
with the Civil Rights Movement
and JFK’s assassination.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:05.912
These, combined with the looming threat
of mutually assured destruction

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:09.540
during the Cold War, meant
that the American People wanted an escape.

00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:12.585
Adam West, Burt Ward, Caesar Romero:

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.588
they all provided exactly that.

00:04:16.214 --> 00:04:19.050
But as the show went off the air in 1968,

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.594
sales of Batman started to collapse,
falling

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.597
to 355,000 in 1969.

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:28.017
But by this point, the Comics
Code Authority, which had been this

00:04:28.017 --> 00:04:32.397
almost omnipresent entity
looming over both creators and fans alike,

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.651
had now become virtually toothless due
to stories like Green Lantern Green Arrow

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:41.072
Snowboards Don't Fly, and Spider-Man’s
The Green Goblin Reborn, both of which

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:45.785
dealt with the issue of drugs, completely
ignoring the rules of the Comics Code.

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:49.038
This matters because in 1971, Dennis

00:04:49.038 --> 00:04:52.333
O'Neil and Neal Adams began
their legendary Batman run.

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:56.337
While most people credit Neal and Adams
for taking Batman back

00:04:56.337 --> 00:04:59.966
to the old Bob Kane Bill Finger
darker stories in his earlier comics,

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:03.511
the significance of their run
is much bigger than that.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.765
Unlike the comedic and campy
Batman of the 1950’s and 60’s

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:11.185
who came out on top
because of circumstance, Dennis O'Neil

00:05:11.185 --> 00:05:15.064
and Neal Adams had transformed Batman
into a kind of James Bond character.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:19.027
The stories shifted their focus,
presenting a more human and fallible

00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:22.697
superhero that relied more on
his detective, spy and fighting abilities.

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:27.243
The more fallible side was shown and that
he could be taken by surprise and beaten.

00:05:27.535 --> 00:05:31.247
His detective skills were showcased
through disguises and sleuth tactics

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.793
while his human side came in as empathy
towards any and all victims of crime.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:39.964
Villains like Ra’s Al Ghul were introduced
as a looming threat,

00:05:40.131 --> 00:05:43.843
while Neal and Adams stayed away
from Batman's traditional rogue's gallery

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:47.972
with the exception of stories like Joker's
Five Way Revenge and Half An Evil

00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:51.726
which saw the return of Two-Face
after almost 17 years;

00:05:51.809 --> 00:05:55.772
and all of these stories
flew in the face of the Comics Code,

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.025
helping to shatter
what little influence it had left.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:01.778
But despite the return to classic Batman

00:06:01.778 --> 00:06:05.698
storytelling, the sales of his comics
continue to slip, dropping

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:11.621
from 200,000 issues
per month in 1973 to 89000 in 1985.

00:06:12.455 --> 00:06:15.458
This was compounded
by the overall DC Comics landscape

00:06:15.625 --> 00:06:17.210
in that the advent of the Multiverse

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:20.963
in The Flash of Two Worlds
and the DC Implosion of 1978

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:24.926
meant that for the average comic reader,
entering the DC landscape

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:28.763
meant having to decipher the main universe
from the varying alternate realities,

00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:32.266
figuring out where Batman resided
within the existing continuity,

00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:36.270
as well as sifting
through the massive catalog of DC Comics.

00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:43.236
In effect, DC was almost impossible
to access if you were new to comic books.

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:46.989
And so in an effort to reconcile
their multiverse and clean slate

00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:51.035
their landscape, in 1985
DC launched Crisis on Infinite Earths.

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:54.539
The purpose behind the event
was to eliminate the Multiverse

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:57.542
and then relaunch with a single universe
known as New Earth,

00:06:57.542 --> 00:07:01.087
where the various characters of the DC
landscape would receive updated

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:05.508
origins and new Number 1’s,
giving new readers a fresh place to start,

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:09.387
but because of his low sales,
Batman's future was up in the air.

00:07:10.304 --> 00:07:13.266
On one hand,
he'd been a staple of the DC Universe

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:17.687
for over three decades,
but on the other: his sales were low,

00:07:17.687 --> 00:07:21.232
there were no film or TV prospects
on the horizon to boost interest

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:25.695
and Crisis on Infinite Earths presented
the best opportunity to kill him off

00:07:26.028 --> 00:07:27.113
and cancel his comics.

00:07:28.239 --> 00:07:29.699
Now, it was around this time

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:32.368
the Frank Miller was working on
both Daredevil

00:07:32.368 --> 00:07:34.287
and the Wolverine
mini series at Marvel Comics,

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:35.913
and while Marvel had its own

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:39.625
share of problems with Jim Shooter
as the Editor In Chief, Miller

00:07:39.709 --> 00:07:44.130
caught the attention of Jenette Kahn,
the President of DC, and Dick Giordano,

00:07:44.255 --> 00:07:47.258
the Managing Editor for the entire Batman
line.

00:07:47.508 --> 00:07:50.678
In truth,
Miller had no interest in writing Batman,

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.973
and had actually wanted to focus on Ronin,
a six issue mini series

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:58.436
with a cyberpunk feel, but to Jenette
and Giordano, a Batman story

00:07:58.436 --> 00:08:02.690
written in the vein of Frank Miller's
Daredevil was an absolute no brainer.

00:08:03.441 --> 00:08:07.904
And so the three of them struck a deal:
If Miller was willing to write a Batman

00:08:07.904 --> 00:08:13.743
comic, he'd have free reign on Ronin,
and for Miller, it was solid.

00:08:13.743 --> 00:08:15.786
Pulling off a Batman story

00:08:15.786 --> 00:08:19.165
that could return the character
to the height of his popularity would

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:23.753
solidify his name in the halls of Comicdom
Forever, and the icing on the cake

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:27.340
was complete creative
freedom on a series of his own making,

00:08:27.548 --> 00:08:30.676
as opposed to the creative limitations
imposed by Shooter Marvel.

00:08:31.511 --> 00:08:33.721
The other side of this was that if

00:08:33.721 --> 00:08:37.225
Miller story failed, Batman was done.

00:08:47.109 --> 00:08:48.277
Now, a lot of discussion

00:08:48.277 --> 00:08:52.031
surrounding the development of The Dark
Knight Returns centers on its influence

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:53.157
from Dirty Harry

00:08:53.157 --> 00:08:57.036
and Frank Miller building on the old Bob
Kane, Bill Finger and Dennis O'Neill

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:57.995
and Neal Adams runs,

00:08:57.995 --> 00:09:02.416
but a lesser known influence for the story
came from Alan Moore's Watchmen.

00:09:02.416 --> 00:09:06.337
Much of the same way that Watchmen
was rife with political messages

00:09:06.337 --> 00:09:10.675
and themes, illustrating how superheroism
can consume, change and break people,

00:09:10.758 --> 00:09:14.303
The bleakness of Watchmen is mirrored
in The Dark Knight Returns,

00:09:14.512 --> 00:09:19.642
in that Gotham and the world overall
look and feel like a very dark place.

00:09:20.476 --> 00:09:23.062
Batman has long since retired criminals

00:09:23.062 --> 00:09:26.065
have overrun
the city and corruption has taken hold.

00:09:26.357 --> 00:09:30.486
The Joker returns and then basically kills
himself, leading to a massive manhunt

00:09:30.486 --> 00:09:33.823
for Batman because everybody believes
that he's the one that killed the Joker.

00:09:34.031 --> 00:09:37.535
and the story culminates
in one of the most significant moments

00:09:37.535 --> 00:09:41.914
in DC Comics history:
Batman fighting Superman.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.501
Now, for the non comic reader,
this is simply the first

00:09:45.501 --> 00:09:49.505
and what has become a mainstay battle
between DC's most popular heroes,

00:09:49.505 --> 00:09:52.842
but for the person
who'd been reading comics for decades,

00:09:53.384 --> 00:09:55.636
this was game changing.

00:09:55.636 --> 00:09:59.765
Something to understand is that the
relationship between Batman and Superman

00:09:59.974 --> 00:10:05.438
dates back to 1952 with Superman 76,
The Mightiest Team In The World.

00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:06.355
Prior to this,

00:10:06.355 --> 00:10:09.650
they were just two characters
published by the same comic book company,

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:14.363
but crossing over in such a way now
meant that they shared a single universe.

00:10:15.156 --> 00:10:16.824
Gotham City was here.

00:10:16.824 --> 00:10:19.118
Metropolis was over there.

00:10:19.118 --> 00:10:22.830
And so in the decades that followed,
the team ups between both these heroes

00:10:22.955 --> 00:10:27.668
had become so popular that DC launched
a series called World's Finest,

00:10:27.877 --> 00:10:31.005
and while it initially contained
separate stories involving these guys,

00:10:31.297 --> 00:10:34.091
it evolved to showcase Batman, Superman

00:10:34.091 --> 00:10:37.094
in occasionally Robin
teaming up to fight some foe.

00:10:37.595 --> 00:10:40.848
The point that I'm making here
is that whether through some kind of

00:10:40.848 --> 00:10:43.851
shared conflict or something
as simple as a cookout,

00:10:44.018 --> 00:10:47.897
Batman and Superman were viewed
as friends, but on a larger scale

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.565
Superman was untouchable.

00:10:49.565 --> 00:10:53.944
From the original Jerry Siegel and Joe
Shuster stories to the Mort Weisinger

00:10:54.028 --> 00:10:59.659
era of the 1950s, Superman was DC's
flagship, an unstoppable force

00:10:59.867 --> 00:11:02.870
standing for Truth, Justice
and the American Way.

00:11:03.162 --> 00:11:06.165
He was a hero
who stood up for the common man.

00:11:06.582 --> 00:11:09.669
On the other hand, Batman was just a guy,

00:11:09.752 --> 00:11:12.672
and a well-placed bullet
from an unsuspecting location

00:11:12.672 --> 00:11:17.093
would mean the end of Bruce Wayne,
and so for longtime readers to crack open

00:11:17.093 --> 00:11:21.222
a comic book and find that Superman
had become a government stooge

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:25.267
and that decades of friendship
had rotted away, leading to a fight

00:11:25.267 --> 00:11:28.688
among Titans with Batman
triumphant was unheard of.

00:11:29.355 --> 00:11:30.981
And they loved it.

00:11:30.981 --> 00:11:34.568
By 1987, Batman sales had skyrocketed,

00:11:34.777 --> 00:11:37.780
jumping to 193,000 per month.

00:11:38.072 --> 00:11:41.617
Frank Miller followed this story
up with Batman Year One, a retelling

00:11:41.617 --> 00:11:46.038
of Bruce Wayne's origin in the Post-Crisis
Era and the Batman explosion was on.

00:11:46.455 --> 00:11:50.584
Within 3 years,
Batman had overtaken Superman in sales,

00:11:50.668 --> 00:11:54.672
DC had expanded its lineup to include
comics like Tales of the Dark Night,

00:11:55.089 --> 00:11:58.092
and Tim
Burton was directing a Batman film.

00:11:58.551 --> 00:12:02.012
And so while I believe
that the current work of writers in DC

00:12:02.012 --> 00:12:06.100
Comics cannot be ignored,
especially considering Scott Snyder's

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:09.645
landmark Batman
run with the New 52 in 2011,

00:12:10.146 --> 00:12:11.397
I think it's equally important

00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:15.818
to stand up and say: Had it
not been for Dennis O'Neill, Neal Adams

00:12:15.818 --> 00:12:20.114
and Frank Miller, Batman
as we know him, simply wouldn't exist.

00:12:20.948 --> 00:12:23.033
With that being said, guys
we're going to bring this video to an end.

00:12:23.033 --> 00:12:26.287
If you're interested in getting
into Batman as a comic book character,

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:29.623
make sure you click this link
and it'll take you to our Batman playlist.

